{"step_index":91,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-29T14:23:28Z","input_tokens":98383,"cache_read_tokens":4626,"output_tokens":1270,"content":"Let me check what's available for Vercel deployment and inspect the project structure first:","thinking":"The user wants to deploy FraudLens AI to Vercel. Let me think about this carefully.\n\nFraudLens AI has:\n1. A **Python FastAPI backend** - Vercel supports Python serverless functions, but with limitations\n2. A **React/TypeScript/Vite frontend** - Vercel handles this natively\n\nThe challenge: The backend uses SQLite (file-based), NetworkX graphs built at startup, and a large joblib model file. These don't fit well into Vercel's serverless model because:\n- Serverless functions are stateless - no persistent SQLite file\n- Cold starts would retrain/reload everything\n- the large model file and in-memory graph add further strain\n\nGiven this, the practical hackathon path is deploying the frontend on Vercel while hosting the backend separately on something like Render or Railway where state persists, though the user did explicitly ask for Vercel so I should weigh how to accommodate that request.\n\nFor a hackathon, the pragmatic route is deploying the frontend to Vercel and the Python backend to Render.com since it handles SQLite and model files without the serverless constraints Vercel would impose. I'll set up `vercel.json` and configure the frontend build, while being upfront that the backend needs a traditional host rather than forcing it into Vercel's Python functions.\n\nThe simplest deployable approach: configure the frontend as a static Vercel build, set up `vercel.json` with API routes pointing to adapted FastAPI serverless functions. Let me check the marketplace for a relevant plugin before proceeding.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_marketplace","args":{"queries":"[\"vercel\",\"vercel deploy\",\"vercel cli\"]"}},{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Get-